You can use a loop to run the same code on multiple variables. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int a = 10, b = 20, c = 30;

    // Using a loop to run the same code on multiple variables
    for (auto& var : {a, b, c}) {
        std::cout << "Value of var: " << var << std::endl;
        // Add your code here
        var *= 2; // Example operation
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the loop iterates over the variables `a`, `b`, and `c` and applies the same operation (`var *= 2`) to each of them.

Alternatively, you can use a function to encapsulate the code and then call it for each variable:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

void processVariable(int& var) {
    std::cout << "Value of var: " << var << std::endl;
    var *= 2;
}

int main() {
    int a = 10, b = 20, c = 30;

    // Using a loop to run the same code on multiple variables
    for (auto& var : {a, b, c}) {
        processVariable(var);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This approach is more modular and easier to maintain if you need to run the same code on a large number of variables.

